<div class="content">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-label">Deep History Nuggets &mdash; Dr. John's Picks</div>
    <div class="nuggets">
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">10,000 BCE &mdash; Green Sahara</div>
        <h3>When the Sahara Was a Savanna</h3>
        <p>The land we call Niger was once a lush, green savanna teeming with hippos, crocodiles, and cattle. Cave paintings in the Air Mountains document this lost world &mdash; proof that African civilizations flourished where only sand dunes stand today. Climate change, not conquest, erased it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">1449 CE &mdash; Sultanate of Agadez</div>
        <h3>Agadez: The Paris of the Sahara</h3>
        <p>The Sultanate of Agadez was the Tuareg world's crown jewel &mdash; a crossroads where salt from Bilma, gold from the south, and manuscripts from Timbuktu all changed hands. Its iconic mud-brick minaret, the tallest mud structure in the world, still stands. UNESCO calls it a World Heritage Site.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">1464&ndash;1591 CE &mdash; Songhai Empire</div>
        <h3>The Songhai Empire: Africa's Forgotten Superpower</h3>
        <p>At its peak under Askia Muhammad I (1493&ndash;1528), the Songhai Empire stretched 2,000 miles across West Africa &mdash; larger than Western Europe. Gao, its capital on the Niger River, was a city of 75,000 when London had 50,000. It fell not to Europeans, but to Moroccan cannons in 1591.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">1591 CE &mdash; Battle of Tondibi</div>
        <h3>The Day Morocco Destroyed the Greatest African Empire</h3>
        <p>Morocco's Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur sent 4,000 soldiers armed with gunpowder across the Sahara to destroy Songhai. Outnumbered 10-to-1 but armed with muskets against spears. Songhai fell, Timbuktu was sacked, and 700,000 manuscripts scattered. This is the history they never taught.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">700&ndash;1900 CE &mdash; Kanem-Bornu</div>
        <h3>Kanem-Bornu: 1,000 Years of African Kingship</h3>
        <p>Eastern Niger sat at the heart of the Kanem-Bornu Empire &mdash; founded around 700 CE and surviving until 1900. It maintained diplomatic relations with North Africa, the Ottoman Empire, and 16th-century Europe. A thousand years of Black sovereignty.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="nugget">
        <div class="nugget-era">1890&ndash;1960 CE &mdash; French Colonization</div>
        <h3>How France Deliberately Kept Niger Poor</h3>
        <p>When France colonized Niger, they inherited one of the wealthiest trade corridors in West Africa &mdash; and systematically dismantled it. Forced labor, extraction without reinvestment, and deliberate suppression of Arabic literacy left a 37% literacy rate at independence. This was policy, not poverty.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-label">Current Events &mdash; What's Happening Now</div>
    <div class="news-grid">
      <div class="news-item">
        <div class="news-dot dot-red"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="news-tag">Political</div>
          <h4>2023 Military Coup &mdash; ECOWAS Standoff Continues</h4>
          <p>In July 2023, Niger's military ousted President Mohamed Bazoum. ECOWAS threatened intervention; it never came. The junta expelled French troops, closed French bases, and pivoted toward Russia's Wagner Group. A new chapter in West African sovereignty &mdash; or a new form of dependency?</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="news-item">
        <div class="news-dot dot-red"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="news-tag">Security</div>
          <h4>IS Sahel Claims Airport Attack in Niamey &mdash; January 2026</h4>
          <p>The Islamic State in the Sahel claimed a deadly attack on Niamey's airport in late January 2026. Rival jihadist groups JNIM and IS Sahel have been fighting each other as well as the government, creating a complex security crisis in the Tillaberi region.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="news-item">
        <div class="news-dot dot-amber"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="news-tag">Economy</div>
          <h4>World Bank Projects 6.7% GDP Growth for 2026&ndash;2027</h4>
          <p>Despite political turmoil, oil production nearing 106,000 barrels per day is driving growth. A $400M oil-backed loan repayment in mid-2026 could reduce extreme poverty by 440,000 people by 2027.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="news-item">
        <div class="news-dot dot-amber"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="news-tag">Geopolitics</div>
          <h4>Uranium Sovereignty: Niger Suspends French Exports</h4>
          <p>Post-coup Niger suspended uranium and gold exports to France. France's nuclear industry depends on Niger's ore and is scrambling for alternatives. African resources for African people.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="news-item">
        <div class="news-dot dot-green"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="news-tag">Culture</div>
          <h4>Agadez Listed Among World's Endangered Heritage Sites</h4>
          <p>The ancient Sultanate of Agadez &mdash; its mosque, mud-brick architecture, Tuareg cultural heritage &mdash; faces climate and conflict threats. A living monument to 600 years of African urban genius.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>  <div class="section">
